('alict_01091', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is an entity in the context of a database?', '["A single column in a table", "A specific SQL query", "A distinct object or thing in the database", "A type of data encryption", "A database index"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "A distinct object or thing in the database". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1091, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01092', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes between an entity and an attribute in a database context?', '["An entity represents a real-world while an attribute is a property that describes the entity", "An entity is always but attributes can be of any data type", "Entities only exist in relational whereas attributes exist in all types of databases", "Attributes are more important than entities because they store the actual data", "Entities describe characteristics of attributes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "An entity represents a real-world while an attribute is a property that describes the entity". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1092, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01093', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In a relation between the entities the type and condition of the relation should be specified. That is called as______attribute', '["Simple attribute", "Composite attribute", "Multivalued attribute", "Key attribute", "Descriptive attribute"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "Descriptive attribute". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1093, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01094', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What does it mean when an attribute is described as "multivalued"?', '["It can take on only one value at any time. th", "It can take on multiple values for each instance of an entity. th", "It is dependent on another attribute for its value. th tys w.h", "It is always null or empty. th", "It must be unique across all instances. th"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: This describes a single-valued not a multivalued attribute. A single-valued attribute can have only one value per entity instance nyq Option 2: This is the correct definition of a multivalued attribute', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1094, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01095', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What type of database system is most suitable for computer-aided design?', '["Relational model", "Flat file model", "Network model", "Object relational model", "Hierarchical model"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A computer-aided design (CAD) system typically involves managing and manipulating complex data such as geometric hierarchies of and relationships among objects. The object relational model is the most suitable for such systems due to the following reasons', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1095, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01096', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the purpose of a composite primary key?', '["To establish a many-to-many relationship between tables. j.=", "To uniquely identify a record using multiple columns", "To enforce data integrity across multiple tables. fndfyda j.=", "To improve query performance by creating a composite index", "To reduce data redundancy by combining related columns"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is 2) To uniquely identify a record using multiple columns. A composite primary key combines two or more columns in a table to form a unique identifier for each record. This is particularly useful when no single column alone can uniquely identify records', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1096, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01097', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In the context of relational which of the following characteristics is unique to many -to-many relationships?', '["Requires each table to have a unique identifier. y\u00f7kd.ekSula", "Requires two foreign keys in one of the original tables", "Needs a separate table to establish the relationship", "Cannot exist without primary keys", "Allows records in both tables to remain independent. j.= foflysu"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the context of relational many -to-many relationships require a separate known as a junction or bridge to establish the relationship between the two original tables', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1097, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01098', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Why would you avoid storing derived attributes directly in a table?', '["To improve query performance", "To avoid redundancy and inconsistency", "To save storage space in the database", "To allow derived attributes to be queried independently", "To enforce referential integrity. fhduq"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Storing derived attributes directly in a table is avoided primarily to prevent redundancy and which is why the correct answer is Option 2. Derived attributes are values that can be calculated or derived from other data in the database', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1098, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01099', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following attributes is not represented in a database table?', '["Single valued attributes", "Multivalued attributes", "Stored attributes", "Key attributes", "Derived attributes"]'::jsonb, 5, 'In a database different types of attributes are represented to store and manage data efficiently. Single- valued attributes (option 1) store only one value per record and are directly present in like a person''s age or name', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1099, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01100', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In the provided is a _________ attribute. What is the best match for the given blank?', '["Simple", "Descriptive", "Composite", "Derived", "Key"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In this the attribute is considered descriptive because it provides additional information about the interaction between the user and the movie', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1100, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, unit, unit_title, question_text, options, correct_option, explanation, latex_formula, trap_insight, difficulty, topic, past_paper_year, past_paper_number, series)
VALUES
('alict_01101', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'A school database tracks and classes. If a student can enroll in multiple classes and a class can have multiple what type of relationship exists between Students and Classes?', '["One-to-One", "One-to-Many", "Many-to-Many", "Ternary", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Students can enroll in multiple Classes. For a student might take and History. Classes can have multiple Students. For a Math class might have 30 students', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1101, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01102', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the main difference between generalization and specialization in EER diagrams?', '["Generalization combines specialization divides an entity", "Generalization enforces primary key specialization enforces foreign key constraints", "Generalization creates weak specialization creates strong entities", "Generalization adds specialization removes attributes", "Generalization is used for specialization is used for attributes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: Generalization is a bottom-up process where two or more entities with common attributes are combined into a higher-level generalized entity. Car and Truck can be generalized into Vehicle', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1102, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01103', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the following relation about an Employee working in a Department at a company. iud.ul fiajh Dept_ID) EID - the unique employee identification number wkkH fiajl wxlh ENIC - the national identity card number of the employee fiajlhdf.a wxlh EName - the name of the employee fiajlhdf.a ku EPhone - a phone number of the employee fiajlhdf.a wxlh Dept_ID - the unique identifier of the department in which the employee works fiajlhd fiajh y÷kd.ekSu Which of the following are jkafka@ A. EID can be the primary key', '["A only. A", "C only. C", "B and C only. B C", "A and C only. A C", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Statement Since EID uniquely identifies each it satisfies the requirement for a primary key. Statement The ENIC (National Identity Card number) is also unique for every making it a candidate key', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1103, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01104', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'The relational schema of the "Employee" table is given below. "Employee"', '["Redundancy", "Data duplication", "Insertion anomaly", "Update anomaly", "Deletion anomaly"]'::jsonb, 5, 'The DepartmentID in the Employee table might link to a specific department. If an employee belongs to a and you delete that it''s possible for Department_Name and Department_Description related to that employee to be accidentally deleted as well. fiajl j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1104, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01105', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following best defines a relational schema?', '["A blueprint that defines the structure of a relation in terms of attributes and their data types", "A physical storage representation of database tables", "A sequence of operations performed on the database", "A backup plan for restoring database instances", "A user interface for interacting with the database"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A relational schema is a logical blueprint or design that describes the structure of a relation (table) in a relational database. It specifies • Attributes The columns or fields in the table. • Data types The type of data that each attribute holds', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1105, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01106', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is NOT true about a database schema?', '["It defines the logical structure of the database. th", "It includes information about and keys", "It changes dynamically with every database transaction", "It provides a blueprint for the database. th", "It includes data types and relationships between tables. thg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A database schema represents the logical structure and blueprint of the database. It is typically static and does not change dynamically with every database transaction. it remains consistent unless explicitly modified by database administrators or developers', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1106, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01107', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Your database stores employee data redundantly in different tables. You identify inconsistencies in an employee''s salary after modifying it in only one table. Which strategy would best solve this issue?', '["A only. A", "C only. C", "A and C only. A C", "A and B only. A B", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "C only. C". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1107, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01108', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Imagine you''re a database designer working on a hospital management system. For patient what field would best serve as a foreign key if linking treatments to specific doctors?', '["Patient ID", "Treatment ID", "Doctor ID", "Appointment Date", "Medical Record Number"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "Doctor ID". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1108, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01109', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following ensures data integrity in a relational database?', '["Using a primary key and foreign key relationship", "Normalizing the database beyond the third normal form", "Using a single table for all records", "Indexing every column in every table", "Avoiding NULL values in any column"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: A primary key uniquely identifies each record in a and a foreign key establishes relationships between ensuring referential integrity. This is a fundamental mechanism for maintaining data integrity in relational databases', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1109, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01110', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the following about database relationships. Which of the following statements are correct?', '["A and B only. A B", "A and C only. A C", "B and C only. B C", "All of the above", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Statement This is true. This is a fundamental concept in relational databases. A primary key uniquely identifies a row within a table. When this same key is used in another table to reference the primary it becomes a foreign key. Statement This is true', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1110, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01111', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the purpose of a unique constraint?', '["To ensure that a column or a combination of columns contains unique values", "To establish a relationship between two tables. j.= folla", "To enforce referential integrity", "To create indexes on a table", "To optimize query performance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: Correct. Ensures no duplicate values in a column or column combination. Option 2: Incorrect. Achieved using foreign not unique constraints. Option 3: Incorrect. Done through foreign not unique constraints. Option 4: Incorrect', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1111, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01112', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the role of a fact table in a data warehouse?', '["To store normalized data from operational systems", "To provide a central table for analytical measures and aggregations", "To define hierarchies between different dimensions", "To ensure referential integrity between dimensions. udkhka", "To reduce the complexity of transactional data models. .kqfokq"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a data the fact table plays a crucial role in storing quantitative data that is essential for analysis. It contains measurable facts or such as or inventory which can be used for reporting and analysis', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1112, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01113', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is true about a foreign key?', '["A foreign key can reference a non-unique column in the parent table", "A foreign key can reference a primary key or a unique key in the parent table", "A foreign key must have the same name as the referenced column in the parent table", "A foreign key can reference multiple columns in the parent table", "A foreign key is automatically indexed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: False. A foreign key must reference a column that has unique such as a primary key or unique in the parent table. Option 2: True. This is a fundamental rule in database design. Option 3: False. The column names can be different as long as the data types and values match', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1113, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01114', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the main benefit of partitioning a table in a database?', '["Reducing the need for indexes", "Minimizing the use of primary keys", "Enhancing query performance and manageability of large datasets", "Simplifying table relationships. j.=", "Allowing unlimited concurrent users"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: partitioning complements indexes but doesn''t replace them. fldgia Option 2: primary keys remain essential. Option 3: partitioning improves performance and simplifies management of large tables', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1114, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01115', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following represents a "Many-to-One" relationship in a relational database?', '["Many students belong to one school fndfyda isiqka", "A teacher can teach multiple courses", "A book can have many authors", "An employee works in one but a department has many employees fiajlfhla", "A customer can have multiple orders"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is 1) Many students belong to one school because a "Many -to-One" relationship in a relational database means multiple records in one table (students) are associated with a single record in another table (school)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1115, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01116', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'When mapping a relationship from an ER diagram to a relational how is the relationship handled?', '["A single foreign key is added to one of the participating entities", "A new table is created with only the primary keys of the participating entities as its attributes. kj idokq ,nkafka", "A new table is created that includes the primary keys of both entities as foreign keys and any attributes of the relationship", "The relationship is stored as a derived attribute in one of the participating entities", "The relationship is split into two relationships"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: This applies to not because one foreign key cannot represent multiple matches on both sides. Option 2: Partially but it overlooks the possibility of the relationship having additional attributes', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1116, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01117', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following database system model is in accordance with Parent -Child relationship in Parent-Child iuqod 1) Explanation of each option', '["D + D\u0305 + C\u0305", "1", "D + D\u0305 + C", "0", "DC + C\u0305 + C"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "D + D̅ + C̅". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1117, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01118', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is an example of a "Many-to-Many" relationship in a relational database?', '["A student can enroll in many and each course can have many students fndfyda", "A car has one owner", "A book can be written by many and an author can write many books", "A manager can manage only one department", "A city has many zip codes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: This is the best example of a Many-to-Many relationship. In a this relationship is often implemented using a join table "Enrollments") that links the primary key of the Students table with the primary key of the Courses table', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1118, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01119', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following illustrates a "Many-to-Many" relationship in a relational database?', '["A student can enroll in many and each course can have many students fndfyda", "A car has one owner", "A library has many books", "An employee works in many departments", "A department has many employees"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "A student can enroll in many and each course can have many students fndfyda". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1119, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01120', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements are correct regarding the mapping of the ER diagram given below into a relational schema?', '["is added to a third table instead of the Employee table. fiajl", "The relationship is mapped to a table with foreign keys referencing Employee and Project including HoursWorked as an attribute", "The Employee table includes both and as composite keys. Employee folu", "is added as a column ot the Employee table. fiajl", "The relationship is mapped to a table with foreign keys referencing Employee and Project and HoursWorked is added to the Project table as an attribute"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "The relationship is mapped to a table with foreign keys referencing Employee and Project including HoursWorked as an attribute". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1120, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01121', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the purpose of an "index" in a database?', '["To speed up data retrieval operations", "To reduce data redundancy", "To create backup copies of the database", "To encrypt data in the database", "To delete unused data from the database"]'::jsonb, 1, 'To speed up data retrieval operations. Indexes are specifically designed to improve the speed of data retrieval by allowing the database to quickly locate data without scanning every row in a table. This significantly enhances query performance and reduces access time for specific records', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1121, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01122', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the benefit of using multiple tables in a database?', '["Faster data entry", "Improved data redundancy", "Easier data organization and relationship modeling. myiq", "Simplified queries", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Using multiple tables in a database offers the benefit of better organization and efficient modeling of relationships between data. By separating information into related redundancy is and data consistency is maintained', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1122, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01123', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What validation should be included when updating a database record?', '["Ensure fields are unique", "Ensure fields are not empty", "Check for duplicate rows", "Verify user permissions", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Option 1: Fields like primary keys or unique constraints should not have duplicate values. For usernames or email addresses must remain unique. Option 2: Required fields should be validated to ensure they are not left blank. This avoids incomplete or invalid records being saved', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1123, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01124', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is typically required to connect a program to a database?', '["API key", "CSS file", "file", "Encryption key", "Database driver"]'::jsonb, 5, 'To connect a program to a database driver is typically required. A database driver acts as a translator between the application and the enabling them to communicate', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1124, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01125', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following correctly defines the cardinality and degree of a respectively?', '["Cardinality defines maximum participation of each and Degree is the number of entities in a relationship", "Cardinality is the number of entity sets in a and Degree is the number of participating instances", "Cardinality is the number of attributes of each and Degree is the number of entities", "Cardinality is the number of participating entity and Degree is the number of strong entities in a relationship", "Cardinality is the number of and Degree is the number of entity instances"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cardinality refers to the maximum participation of each entity in the defined in terms like "one-to-one", "one-to-many" or "many-to-many." jeks jpk Degree refers to the number of entities or entity sets involved in the relationship', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1125, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01126', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements is correct about database models?', '["Relational model has data integrity issues", "Hierarchical model can handle any type of relationship", "Network model provides data independence whereas hierarchical model does not", "Network model allows to inherit objects such as tables", "Flat file systems allow to retrieve data efficiently"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: This is incorrect. The relational model is specifically designed to ensure data integrity through constraints like primary foreign and normalization. Option 2: This is incorrect', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1126, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01127', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is a key characteristic of a weak entity in an ER diagram?', '["It does not have a primary key and depends on a strong entity. thg", "It always has a composite key. tys", "It represents a many-to-many relationship. th", "It cannot have attributes. thg", "It acts as a super key for a table. th"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: A weak entity does not have a primary key of its own. it relies on a strong entity for its identification. A weak entity is uniquely identified using a combination of a foreign key (from the strong entity) and a partial key (an attribute specific to the weak entity)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1127, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01128', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the primary purpose of an ER diagram?', '["To represent database relationships visually", "To design a website layout", "To calculate mathematical operations", "To define coding logic", "To organize server configuration"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The primary purpose of an ER (Entity -Relationship) diagram is to represent database relationships visually. It provides a graphical representation of and the relationships between making it easier to understand and design databa se structures', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1128, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01129', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'If a certain attribute can be represented as a collection of simple what is that attribute called?', '["Stored attributes", "Descriptive attributes", "Composite attributes", "Derived attributes", "Simple attributes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An attribute in a database can be classified into different types based on its complexity and derivation. If an attribute can be divided into multiple simpler it is known as a composite attribute', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1129, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01130', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the name given to a record that uses multiple fields to uniquely identify it?', '["Alternate key", "Foreign key", "Candidate key", "Composite key", "Primary key"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A composite key is a key that consists of two or more fields (attributes) used together to uniquely identify a record in a table. This is required when a single field is not sufficient to ensure uniqueness', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1130, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01131', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What does cardinality indicate in an ER diagram?', '["Data storage requirements", "Entity relationships", "The number of entities involved in a relationship", "Table attributes", "Primary key constraints"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is 3) The number of entities involved in a relationship. In an ER (Entity-Relationship) cardinality indicates the number of instances of one entity that are associated with the instances of another entity in a relationship', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1131, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01132', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the role of a derived attribute in an ER diagram?', '["It is an attribute stored permanently in the database. th", "It is a key used to relate two entities. th", "It is computed from other attributes and not stored in the database. th", "It represents a weak entity th", "It defines the primary key th"]'::jsonb, 3, '• Option 1: Incorrect because derived attributes are not stored permanently in the database. they are calculated when needed. .Kkh • Option 2: Incorrect because a derived attribute is not a key. It does not establish relationships between entities', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1132, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01133', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following describes a "Many-to-Many" relationship in a relational database?', '["One record in a table can relate to multiple records in another table", "Multiple records in the first table can relate to multiple records in the second table through a join table", "Each record in the first table can be related to many records in the same table", "Each record in the first table is related to one and only one record in the second table", "A table can only have a one-to-one relationship with another table"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: Incorrect. This describes a one -to-many where a single record in the first table can have multiple related records in the second table. For a customer placing multiple orders demonstrates a one-to-many relationship. nyq Option 2: Correct', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1133, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01134', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is a part of data manipulation language (DML) of SQL used in relational database?', '["CREATE", "INSERT", "ALTER", "DROP", "GRANT"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Data Manipulation Language (DML) in SQL is used to manipulate or manage the data within tables in a relational database', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1134, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01135', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements is true about normalization in a database?', '["Normalization is used to increase data redundancy", "Normalization is used to encrypt data", "Normalization is used to back up data", "Normalization is used to combine all data into a single table", "Normalization is used to reduce data redundancy"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "Normalization is used to reduce data redundancy". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1135, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01136', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is called by the aliases of table countability of a data hk ,nkafka : 3 ) In the context of the terms "table size" and "countability of a data table" generally refer to the number of rows or entries within a table. Each row in a table is referred to as a iuqodhkays ikao¾Nh table size .Kka yelshdj hk mo idudkHfhka fhduq .Kk Field', '["F = A. B + A\u0305. B\u0305", "F = (A + B). (A\u0305. B\u0305)", "F = (A + B\u0305). (A\u0305 + B)", "F = A. B\u0305 + A\u0305. B", "F = A. B + A. B\u0305\u0305\u0305\u0305\u0305"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "F = A. B + A̅. B̅". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1136, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01137', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following correctly represents a weak entity?', '["It has a primary key", "It does not depend on any other entity th", "It has a partial key and depends on a strong thg", "It has only relationships but no attributes thg", "It cannot participate in a relationship thg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: Incorrect. A weak entity does not have a primary key of its own. it has a partial key when combined with the primary key of a strong forms a unique identifier. Option 2: Incorrect. A weak entity must depend on a strong entity for identification', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1137, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01138', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is a type of database management system', '["1 and 0", "0 and 1", "and 1", "and", "None of above"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The correct answer is 4) All of the above because and Oracle are all types of Database Management Systems Each of these systems is used to and interact with though they vary in features and capabilities', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1138, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01139', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following best represents total participation in an EER model?', '["Every instance of an entity must participate in at least one relationship", "Some instances of an entity may not participate in any relationships", "An entity must have a key attribute for total participation", "A relationship must include all entities in the database", "Participation is restricted to entities in subclasses only"]'::jsonb, 1, '• Option 1: In an Enhanced Entity-Relationship (EER) total participation means that all instances of an entity are required to participate in at least one relationship with another entity in the specified relationship type', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1139, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01140', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'When converting an ER diagram to a relational igykla .nvdjla is a one-to-many relationship typically represented in tables?', '["By adding a column with the entity name", "By creating separate tables with a foreign key in one of them. tll", "By combining all entities into a single table", "By duplicating entity attributes across multiple tables. j.= lsysmhla", "By removing relationships entirely"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a relational a one -to-many relationship is typically represented by creating separate tables where one of the tables contains a foreign key that links to the primary key of the other table. This approach is best described by Option 2', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1140, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01141', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following scenarios represents redundancy in a database?', '["An \"Order\" table contains multiple records of orders placed by the same customer. \"Order\" tlu", "A customer name appears as a foreign key in an \"Invoice\" table. \"Invoice\"", "A table has missing rows for some customers who didn''t make a purchase", "A database stores customer contact information in both the \"Customer\" and \"Order\" tables", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Redundancy in a database occurs when the same data is unnecessarily duplicated across multiple tables. In Option 4, customer contact information is stored in both the "Customer" and "Order" which is an example of redundancy', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1141, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01142', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the primary cause of data anomalies?', '["Proper normalization", "Use of complex queries", "Poor database design", "High data redundancy", "Excessive foreign keys"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is 3) because poor database design is the primary cause of data anomalies. When a database is not designed it can result in issues such as and delete where inconsistent or redundant data is created', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1142, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01143', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is a type of database model?', '["Hierarchical", "Relational", "Object-oriented", "Network", "All of the What will be the output of the following code?"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The code initializes the variable start to -10 and end to -5. It then enters a while loop that runs as long as start is less than end. Inside the code checks if start is divisible by 3 using the condition start % 3 == 0. If it prints the value of start followed by a comma and space', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1143, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01144', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What happens if a table is in 3NF?', '["All non-key attributes are fully dependent on the primary key", "The table is free from both partial and transitive dependencies", "There is no need to check for candidate keys", "Data redundancy is increased for better performance", "All attributes are functionally dependent on the entire primary key"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: This statement refers to not 3NF. 2NF ensures that all non-key attributes are fully dependent on the entire primary not just part of it. 3NF 2NF fldgila Option 2: This statement is correct for 3NF', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1144, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01145', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which normal form is a relation considered not to have transitive dependencies?', '["Zeroth Normal Form", "First Normal Form", "Second Normal Form", "Third Normal Form", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In database a relation is considered not to have transitive dependencies when it is in the Third Normal Form (3NF). Transitive dependency occurs when a non-prime attribute depends on another non-prime in depends on a candidate key', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1145, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01146', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is a foreign key in a relational database?', '["A key used to uniquely identify a record in a table", "A key that establishes a link between records in two tables. j.= foll", "A key that is used to encrypt data", "A key that indexes the data in a table", "A key that maintains data integrity within a table"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a relational a foreign key is a field or a collection of fields in one table that establishes a link between the records of two tables. It does this by matching the values in a column with the primary key column in another table', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1146, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01147', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the primary role of a Foreign Key Constraint in a relational database?', '["It ensures that each entry in a table is unique. th", "It creates a link between two tables by referencing the primary key of another table. th", "It restricts the type of data that can be entered into a column. th", "It checks the validity of data before it is entered into the database. th", "It allows for duplicate entries in a table if the data type matches"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is 2), as this accurately describes the primary role of a Foreign Key constraint in a relational database. A Foreign Key is used to establish a relationship between two tables by referencing the Primary Key in another table', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1147, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01148', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the main characteristic of stored attributes in a database?', '["They are attributes that are calculated during runtime. Odjk", "They do not exist physically in the database", "They are attributes that are stored in the database as permanent data", "They are always derived from other attributes", "They are optional attributes for the entity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "They are attributes that are stored in the database as permanent data". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1148, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01149', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What type of attribute is represented with an oval that is connected to another oval in an ER diagram?', '["Composite Attribute", "Multivalued Attribute", "Derived Attribute", "Key Attribute", "Weak Attribute"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In an Entity-Relationship (ER) a composite attribute is represented by an oval that is further connected to other ovals. This indicates that the attribute can be divided into smaller each representing a specific component of the composite attribute', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1149, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01150', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the below ER rem igyk is the type of attribute used for the age of the customer?', '["Simple attribute", "Composite attribute", "Multivalued attribute", "Key attributes", "Derived attribute"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "Derived attribute". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1150, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, unit, unit_title, question_text, options, correct_option, explanation, latex_formula, trap_insight, difficulty, topic, past_paper_year, past_paper_number, series)
VALUES
('alict_01151', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is cardinality in database relationships?', '["The number of rows in a table", "The numerical relationship between entities", "The number of columns in a table", "The size of the database", "The number of indexes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cardinality in database relationships refers to the numerical relationship between two entities in an Entity-Relationship (ER) model. It describes how many instances of one entity are associated with instances of another entity', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1151, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01152', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is a foreign key in a database?', '["A field in a table that uniquely identifies each record", "A key used for sorting data in a table", "A field in one table that refers to the primary key in another table", "A temporary storage area in the database", "A key used to encrypt sensitive data"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a foreign key is a field (or a collection of fields) in one table that refers to the primary key in another table. This relationship links the two tables and establishes a connection between the data stored in them', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1152, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01153', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is a candidate key?', '["A foreign key that uniquely identifies a record", "A primary key that consists of multiple columns", "A set of attributes that can uniquely identify a row in a relation", "A key that is used to lock the database", "A key that is used to encrypt the data"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: This is incorrect. A foreign key is a column or set of columns in one table that references the primary key of another table. It is used to establish and enforce a link between the data in two but it does not uniquely identify records', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1153, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01154', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is data redundancy in database terms?', '["Storing the same data multiple times", "Missing data in tables", "Corrupted data", "Encrypted data", "Temporary data"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Data redundancy in database terms refers to the unnecessary duplication of data. This happens when the same piece of data is stored in multiple places within a database', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1154, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01155', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is a composite key?', '["A key that consists of a single column", "A key that consists of multiple columns", "A key that is used to create foreign key relationships", "A key that is used to partition the data", "A key that is used to encrypt the data"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: This is incorrect. This is a simple not a composite key. Option 2: This is correct. A composite key is made up of two or more columns to uniquely identify a record. .Kklska Option 3: This is incorrect', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1155, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01156', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'If a "Customer" table includes the following which one is most likely derived?', '["Customer_ID", "Phone_Number", "Email_Address", "Customer_Age", "Registration_Date"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "Customer_Age". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1156, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01157', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements about relational databases is true?', '["Primary keys in tables are selected from alternate keys", "A table cannot have more than one alternate key in addition to the primary key", "A primary key is selected from candidate keys", "All possible keys are called primary keys. fhdod", "Candidate keys in a table are selected from primary keys"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "A primary key is selected from candidate keys". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1157, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01158', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'You are given the task of converting an ER diagram to a relational schema. What should you do first?', '["Define the domain for each attribute", "Convert entities and their attributes into tables", "Identify candidate keys for all attributes", "Specify the relationships between tables using foreign keys", "Mark alternate keys for future use"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When transitioning from an ER diagram to a relational the process begins with the basic structural ER igykl isg • Entities in the ER diagram are mapped to tables in the schema', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1158, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01159', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In which situation would it make the most sense to use a ternary relationship?', '["When three entities are connected in a single relationship", "When one entity is linked to multiple instances of another entity", "When two entities have shared attributes but remain distinct", "When an entity has several related attributes that can be grouped together", "When an entity does not directly relate to other entities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: This is the correct situation for using a ternary relationship. It occurs when three entities interact and their relationship cannot be represented effectively using separate binary relationships', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1159, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01160', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the result of mapping a one-to-one relationship from an ER diagram to a relational schema?', '["The relationship is mapped to a separate table with foreign keys for both entities", "The relationship is ignored as it has no impact on the schema. th", "One entity''s primary key becomes a foreign key in the other entity''s table", "A composite attribute is added to one of the entities", "A multivalued attribute is created in both entities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: This approach is used for complex relationships but is generally not required for one-to-one unless there are additional attributes specific to the relationship. Option 2: This is incorrect. A one-to-one relationship does impact the schema', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1160, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01161', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider a relational schema with the attributes "Employee_ID", "Name", "Department_ID", and "Department_Name". If "Department_ID" is a foreign which statement is true?', '["It uniquely identifies each record in the Employee table. th Employee", "It connects the Employee table to the Department table. th Employee Department", "It is used as a candidate key for the Employee table. th Employee", "It defines the domain for the Department table. th Department", "It acts as an alternate key in the Employee table. th Employee"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: Incorrect. This describes a primary not a foreign key. Option 2: Correct. A foreign key creates a link between the Employee table and the Department table. Option 3: Incorrect. A foreign key is not a candidate Option 4: Incorrect', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1161, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01162', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is true about indexes in a database?', '["Indexes are used to enforce constraints", "Indexes improve the speed of data retrieval operations", "Indexes increase the size of the database but slow down queries", "Indexes can only be created on primary keys", "Indexes duplicate the entire table for faster access"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: Indexes do not enforce integrity. Option 2: True. Indexes improve the speed of data retrieval by allowing the database to locate rows faster without scanning the entire table. j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1162, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01163', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In a "Sales" if TotalRevenue is a derived what might cause its calculation to fail?', '["Missing data in", "Invalid entries in", "Null values in Quantity or Price fields", "Foreign key violation in", "Redundant records in Sales table"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A only. A 2) 3) 4) All of the above. 5) None of the above. Option 1: Irrelevant to the calculation of TotalRevenue. TotalRevenue Option 2: This is also irrelevant since TotalRevenue depends on Quantity and Price. Quantity Price Option 3: Correct', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1163, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01164', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is a database schema?', '["The physical files storing the data", "The structure that defines and relationships in a database", "A temporary table used for queries", "A collection of user privileges", "A backup file of the database"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: A database schema is not about physical storage or Option 2: It defines the design of data and relationships. th Option 3: Temporary tables are unrelated to Option 4: While user privileges can be associated with a they do not define it', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1164, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01165', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What does the term "cardinality" refer to in database design?', '["The number of rows in a table", "The relationship between tables", "The uniqueness of data values in a column", "The number of primary keys in a table", "The total size of a database in bytes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: Cardinality does not refer to the number of Option 2: The relationship between tables refers to types of relationships (one etc.), which is cardinality. j.= Option 3: Uniqueness of data values in a column relates to constraints like unique not cardinality', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1165, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01166', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What happens when a foreign key constraint is violated?', '["The database automatically deletes the related records", "The database automatically updates the related records", "The database rejects the insertion or update operation", "The database generates a runtime error", "The database ignores the constraint violation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'CB+A 2) (CB+0)A 3) CBA 4) 4. CBA̅̅̅̅̅̅ 5) A Option 1: This does not happen unless a DELETE CASCADE is explicitly defined for the foreign key. Option 2: This occurs only if ON UPDATE CASCADE is which is not the default behavior', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1166, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01167', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is not a characteristic of flat file which is a database architecture?', '["All data is stored in a single table", "Finding data is difficult", "Difficulty changing the type of data entered", "Updating is easy", "The possibility of writing the same data over and over again is high. tlu"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Option 1: This is true for flat file where data lacks a relational structure. Option 2: Without indexing or relational searching for specific data in a flat file is inefficient. Option 3: Modifying the schema or data type is cumbersome because flat files lack flexibility', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1167, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01168', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is an Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram used for in database design?', '["To visualize the structure of a database", "To generate code automatically", "To analyze network traffic", "To send emails", "To design user interfaces"]'::jsonb, 1, '• Statement An ER diagram is a visual representation of the entities (tables) in a database and their relationships. It helps in designing the database schema by depicting how data entities interact with each which is crucial for understanding the database structure and planning its design', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1168, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01169', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the primary key of the new table created for a multivalued attribute?', '["A composite key consisting of the primary key of the original entity and a unique identifier for the multivalued attribute", "The primary key of the original entity", "A unique identifier for the multivalued attribute. nyq", "A combination of the primary keys of two entities", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: When a multivalued attribute is transformed into a separate table during database the new table must uniquely identify each occurrence of the multivalued attribute for a specific entity instance', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1169, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01170', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In a normalized which of the following is not true for the Second Normal Form (2NF)?', '["It eliminates partial dependency", "All non-key attributes are fully dependent on the primary key", "It eliminates transitive dependency", "It requires the database to first meet the requirements of the First Normal Form (1NF)", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is 3) It eliminates transitive as this is not true for the Second Normal Form (2NF). In a normalized 2NF focuses on eliminating partial meaning all non -key attributes must be fully dependent on the primary key', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1170, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01171', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which attribute is used to store the number of hours each student plays each game?', '["Stored Attributes", "Composite Attributes", "Descriptive Attributes", "Derived Attributes", "Simple Attributes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Descriptive Attributes These are attributes that provide additional information or describe a relationship between entities', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1171, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01172', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following SQL queries will return only unique rows based on multiple ensuring that duplicates are excluded?', '["SELECT DISTINCT column1, column2 FROM", "SELECT DISTINCT(column1, column2) FROM", "SELECT column1, column2 FROM table_name GROUP BY column1,", "SELECT column1, column2 FROM table_name WHERE DISTINCT column1,", "SELECT UNIQUE column1, column2 FROM"]'::jsonb, 1, 'SELECT DISTINCT column1, column2 FROM This is correct. The DISTINCT keyword ensures that only unique combinations of column1 and column2 are returned', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1172, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01173', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL statement will correctly update the tax rate to 15% for employees whose salary is in the employees table?', '["MODIFY employees UPDATE tax_rate = ''15%'' WHERE salary", "UPDATE employees SET tax_rate = 0.15 WHERE salary ==", "UPDATE employees SET tax_rate = 0.15 WHERE salary", "UPDATE tax_rate SET tax_rate = 0.15 WHERE employees.salary", "UPDATE * SET tax_rate = 0.15 WHERE employees.salary =="]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "UPDATE employees SET tax_rate = 0.15 WHERE salary". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1173, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01174', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following correctly describes the difference between INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN in SQL?', '["INNER JOIN returns all while LEFT JOIN filters rows based on conditions. INNER JOIN", "LEFT JOIN includes unmatched rows from the right table. LEFT JOIN", "INNER JOIN only includes rows that have matching values in both tables. INNER JOIN", "LEFT JOIN excludes rows with NULL values in the left table. LEFT JOIN", "INNER JOIN excludes duplicate rows while LEFT JOIN does not. INNER JOIN iajhxl%Shj"]'::jsonb, 3, 'INNER JOIN Returns only the rows where there is a match between the columns specified in both tables. If no match is the row is excluded from the result. INNER JOIN j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1174, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01175', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In which normal form is a table if all its attributes are fully functionally dependent on the primary ka 2 ) Explanation of each option', '["NF (First Normal Form", "101.10102", "110.01102", "101.01012", "101. 11102"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "NF (First Normal Form". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1175, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01176', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the correct SQL query to find the number of rows in the Employee table?', '["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM", "SELECT SUM() FROM", "SELECT NUMBER(ROWS) FROM", "SELECT COUNT() TO", "SELECT ROWS() FROM"]'::jsonb, 1, 'COUNT(*) is an SQL aggregate function that returns the total number of rows in a including rows with NULL values. COUNT(*) SQL In this COUNT(*) is applied to the Employee and it counts all the rows in the table', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1176, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01177', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL keyword is used to sort the result set of a SQL query? SQL j¾. SQL moh 2 ) Explanation of each option', '["GROUP BY This is used to group rows that have the same values in specified columns. It is not used for sorting", "ORDER BY The ORDER BY keyword is used in SQL to sort the result set of a query by one or more either in ascending (ASC) or descending (DESC) order. SQL ORDER BY moh", "SORT This is not a valid SQL keyword. Sorting is achieved using ORDER BY", "FILTER This is not a valid SQL keyword. Filtering rows is done using the WHERE not FILTER", "GROUP BY"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "GROUP BY This is used to group rows that have the same values in specified columns. It is not used for sorting". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1177, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01178', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is an example of a unique constraint violation?', '["Inserting a record with a null value in a unique column", "Updating a record with a value that violates a foreign key constraint", "Deleting a record that is referenced by a foreign key in another table", "Inserting a record with a duplicate value in a unique column", "Updating a record with a value that violates a check constraint"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Option 1: This is not a violation because unique columns can usually accept multiple NULL as NULL is treated as a non-comparable entity in most databases. fndfyda Option 2: This refers to a FOREIGN KEY constraint not a UNIQUE constraint violation', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1178, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01179', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the given relation of the following SQL statements is syntactically correct on the above relation?', '["SELECT ALL FROM Employee", "SELECT *.* FROM Employee", "SELECT empno.name FROM Employee", "SELECT * FROM Employee", "SELECT emp.name FROM Employee"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The correct SQL statement that is syntactically correct for the given relation is 4) SELECT * FROM Employee. This statement is used to select all columns from the Employee table. ,nd * FROM Employee. Employee Here''s why the other options are incorrect', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1179, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01180', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following describes a table in 0th Normal Form (0NF)?', '["The table has no repeating groups and all columns are", "The table has atomic but may have repeating", "The table may have repeating groups and no structure", "The table has no transitive dependencies", "The table has no foreign keys"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A table in 0th Normal Form (0NF) is not normalized. 0 jeks It may have repeating groups (non-atomic or sets within columns). There is no structure or constraints applied to ensure the data is organized systematically. thg', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1180, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01181', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'A developer wants to create a database called Which of the following SQL statements should be used to achieve this task?', '["BUILD DATABASE", "NEW SCHEMA", "CONNECT TO", "CREATE TABLE", "CREATE DATABASE"]'::jsonb, 5, 'To create a new database called the correct SQL statement is CREATE DATABASE and SQL Server', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1181, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01182', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the attribute that cannot be use inside the image tag?', '["src", "width", "height", "alt", "controls"]'::jsonb, 5, 'In the Option 1: specifies the path of the image file Option 2: defines the width of the image Option 3: sets the height of the image Option 4: provides alternative text for the image if it cannot be displayed', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1182, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01183', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is NOT a type of JOIN operation in SQL?', '["INNER JOIN", "OUTER JOIN", "CROSS JOIN", "LEFT JOIN", "BACK JOIN"]'::jsonb, 5, 'BACK JOIN is not a recognized or standard type of JOIN operation in SQL. The other options are valid types of JOIN operations commonly used in SQL. BACK JOIN SQL • INNER Returns rows when there is a match in both tables. j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1183, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01184', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In the context of what is an anomaly?', '["An unusual pattern found in data through analysis tools", "A type of database corruption that causes tables to become unreadable. j.=", "An error that occurs when incorrect relationships are defined in the schema", "A problem that arises due to redundancy or poor database affecting data integrity", "A security vulnerability that allows unauthorized access to sensitive data"]'::jsonb, 4, '1) . B + D + A̅ 2) . A + B̅D 3) . AC + D 4) . A + C̅ + D 5) . AB + B̅D An anomaly in the context of databases refers to a problem that arises due to redundancy or poor database affecting data integrity', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1184, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01185', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What does the term "atomic" mean in the context of database normalization?', '["Each attribute contains a set of values", "Each attribute contains indivisible values", "Attributes can contain nested tables", "Each table contains multiple primary keys", "A column is required for every possible value yels"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the context of database "atomic" refers to the principle that each attribute in a table should contain values that are indivisible or cannot be further broken down. This ensures that each cell in a table has a specific value rather than a or composite value. w', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1185, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01186', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the correct SQL code for applying the UNIQUE constraint to the age column of a table created as student?', '["ALTER TABLE student UNIQUE", "ALTER TABLE student ADD age", "ALTER TABLE student ADD CONSTRAINT uniqueage UNIQUE", "ALTER TABLE student CONSTRAINT uniqueage UNIQUE", "ALTER TABLE student CREATE CONSTRAINT uniqueage UNIQUE"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "ALTER TABLE student ADD CONSTRAINT uniqueage UNIQUE". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1186, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01187', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the primary goal of normalization in databases?', '["Increase data redundancy", "Optimize query performance", "Minimize data anomalies and redundancy", "Enhance security features", "Ensure hardware compatibility"]'::jsonb, 3, 'If a student can enroll in multiple and a class can have multiple the relationship between "Students" and "Classes" in a school database is Many-to-Many', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1187, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01188', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL clause is used to filter the results of a lrkafka SQL : 2) Explanation', '["ORDER BY", "WHERE", "GROUP BY", "HAVING", "SELECT 6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "ORDER BY". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1188, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01189', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which constraint allows setting a rule that restricts the values entered in a column based on a logical condition?', '["PRIMARY KEY", "UNIQUE", "DEFAULT", "CHECK", "FOREIGN KEY"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Option 1: A PRIMARY KEY constraint ensures that each row in a table has a unique and non -null value for the specified column or set of columns. It does not enforce logical conditions beyond ensuring uniqueness and non-null constraints. w', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1189, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01190', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the correct SQL code to create a database as Student?', '["CREATE", "CREATE SCHEMA", "CREATE TABLE Student", "CREATE DATABASE", "INSERT INTO"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "CREATE DATABASE". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1190, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01191', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the correct SQL query to retrieve the details of all columns and rows from a table named Students?', '["SELECT * FROM", "GET ALL FROM", "FETCH ALL FROM", "SHOW TABLE", "SELECT ALL FROM"]'::jsonb, 1, 'SELECT * FROM The SELECT * statement in SQL is used to retrieve all columns and rows from a specified table. * is a wildcard character that represents all columns in the table. SQL SELECT * FROM Students specifies the table from which the data should be retrieved', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1191, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01192', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the followings can be considered as the candidate key regarding the student relation?', '["Stu_ID", "Stu_Name", "Gender", "Address", "DOB"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A candidate key is a minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple (row) in a relation. 1) Stu_ID This is typically assigned uniquely to each ensuring that no two students have the same ID. This makes it suitable as a candidate key', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1192, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01193', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL operator is used to match a pattern in a string?', '["LIKE", "MATCH", "FIND", "SEARCH", "SIMILAR"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The LIKE operator in SQL is used to match patterns in strings', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1193, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01194', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is the primary goal of database normalization?', '["Minimizing the number of tables in the database", "Ensuring that all data is stored in a single table", "Eliminating data redundancy and ensuring data integrity", "Speed up query performance by denormalizing tables. j.=", "Ensuring that each table has only one column"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Statement 1 Normalization often leads to the creation of more not as data is separated into related tables to reduce redundancy. Statement 2 This goes against the principles of normalization', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1194, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01195', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the relations answer correctly represents the SQL command to increase the salary of all employees whose DepID = "D101" by 5000. DepID = "D101" fiajlhskaf.a jegqm 5000 lskaa ioyd SQL', '["UPDATE Employee ADD WHERE", "UPDATE INTO Employee SET WHERE", "UPDATE Employee SET", "UPDATE SET From Employee WHERE", "UPDATE Employee SET WHERE"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "UPDATE Employee SET WHERE". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1195, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01196', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is eliminated when a table goes from 1NF to 2NF ?', '["Candidate keys", "Transitive dependencies", "Partial dependencies", "Repeating groups", "Nove of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A table is in 2NF (Second Normal Form) if it satisfies the following • It is in 1NF (First Normal Form) This means that the table should have only atomic (indivisible) and there should be no repeating groups or arrays within a single column. fuhska woyia lrkafka j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1196, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01197', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'In a table the column must always be positive. Which of the following constraints ensures this rule?', '["UNIQUE", "NOT NULL", "DEFAULT 0", "CHECK", "PRIMARY KEY"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Option 1: Ensures that all values in the column are distinct. Does not impose any restriction on whether the values are or zero. Option 2: Ensures that the column cannot have null (empty) values. It does not restrict the value to be or zero', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1197, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01198', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which combination of constraints ensures that a column in a table can never be null and must always contain a unique value?', '["NOT NULL and UNIQUE", "UNIQUE and CHECK", "NOT NULL and DEFAULT", "PRIMARY KEY and CHECK", "FOREIGN KEY and UNIQUE"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: This is correct. The NOT NULL constraint ensures that the column cannot have null (empty) values. The UNIQUE constraint ensures that all values in the column are distinct. these constraints ensure that the column always contains a non-null and unique value', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1198, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01199', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statement is true about following SQL query?', '["Ensures Price can be negative or null. Price", "Ensures ProductName can be null. ProductName", "Ensures Price must always be greater than zero. Price", "Ensures Stock must always have a value. Stock", "Ensures can have duplicate values. g"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The given SQL query creates a table Products where is unique and cannot have ProductName must have a value and cannot be Price is restricted to values greater than zero with a CHECK constraint and Stock has a default value of 0 if no value is provided. ,nd Price w', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1199, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01200', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which type of database is designed to handle large volumes of structured and unstructured often used for applications like data warehousing and big data analytics?', '["Relational database", "database", "Object-oriented database", "Hierarchical database", "Network database"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: Designed for structured data and uses a schema with rows and tables. It is not ideal for handling large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data. Option 2: Designed to handle large volumes of and unstructured data', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1200, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
