('alict_01317', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the correct SQL code for display total of the salary of employees table who get more than', '["Select Count(salary) From Employees Where salary", "Select sum (salary) From Employees Where salary", "Select Total (salary) From Employees like salary", "Select Total (salary) From Employees To salary", "Select From Employees Count(salary) Like salary"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This question is about the language used to get data from a database. m%Yakh It gives you 5 different SQL commands (queries), and you have to choose the one th Tng Looks at a table called Employees Finds only those employees whose salary is more than Rs', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1317, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01318', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following SQL statement', '["A only", "B only", "C only", "A and C only", "B and C only. A B C A C B C 6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "A only". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1318, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01319', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL statement is used to remove the Phone column from the Customer table?', '["DELETE COLUMN Phone FROM", "ALTER TABLE Customer DROP COLUMN", "REMOVE Phone FROM", "ALTER TABLE Customer DELETE", "DROP Phone COLUMN FROM"]'::jsonb, 2, 'To remove (drop) a column from an existing table in the standard SQL syntax SQL ALTER TABLE table_name DROP COLUMN So in this case ALTER TABLE Customer DROP COLUMN This removes the Phone column from the Customer table permanently. Customer j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1319, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01320', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following SQL statements is used to remove a table and all of its data permanently from a database?', '["DROP Permanently removes the table and all its data. Also deletes the structure and can''t be rolled back (in most cases). tys", "DELETE Deletes rows from a table based on a condition. The table structure remains intact. Can be rolled back if used within a transaction. fldkafoaishla", "REMOVE Not a valid SQL command in standard SQL", "TRUNCATE Removes all rows from a but keeps the structure for future use. Faster than DELETE but not the same as DROP", "CLEAR Not a standard SQL command for deleting data or structure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The given code counts the number of vowels in a list of words. ,nd It iterates through each word in the list ["hello", "world", "python", "loop"] and checks each character to see if it is a vowel (from the string "aeiou"). For each vowel the counter cnt is incremented', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1320, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01321', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'How do you query the NAME field for all American cities in the CITY table with populations larger than 120000?', '["SELECT name FROM city WHERE countryCode = \"USA\" and Population", "SELECT * FROM city WHERE countryCode = \"USA\" and Population", "SELECT", "SELECT name FROM city WHERE Population", "SELECT name FROM city WHERE countryCode ="]'::jsonb, 1, 'To query the NAME field for all American cities in the CITY table with populations larger than 120000, you can use the following SQL 120000 g SELECT NAME FROM CITY WHERE AND CountryCode = AND Population This query selects the NAME field from the CITY table where the Population is gr', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1321, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01322', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the role of the SQL HA VING clause?', '["To filter records before grouping", "To filter records after grouping", "To order the result set", "To create a new table", "To delete records"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the HAVING clause is used to filter records after a GROUP BY operation. SQL GROUP BY fufyhqulska miq Unlike the WHERE which filters rows before any the HAVING clause allows you to filter groups based on aggregate functions (like etc.)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1322, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01323', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL keyword is used to combine rows from two or more based on a related column and includes unmatched rows from the left table? j.= follska .Kklska SQL moh" o@ Answer : 2) Explanation of each option', '["INNER JOIN Combines rows from two or more tables where there is a match in the related columns. Only matched rows are included in the result set (no unmatched rows are shown)", "LEFT JOIN (or LEFT OUTER JOIN) Returns all rows from the left along with the matched rows from the right table. If there is no NULL values are returned for columns from the right ensuring unmatched rows from the left table are still included", "RIGHT JOIN (or RIGHT OUTER JOIN) Similar to LEFT but returns all rows from the right along with the matched rows from the left table. Unmatched rows from the right table will still be included with NULL values for columns from the left table. LEFT JOIN g iudk\"", "FULL OUTER JOIN Returns all rows when there is a match in either table. If there is no NULL values are returned for the missing side in both directions (left and right)", "CROSS JOIN Produces a Cartesian product of the two meaning every row from the first table is combined with every row from the second table. There is no condition for matching rows. j.= fofla Cartesian ksIamdokhla ksmojhs\""]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "LEFT JOIN (or LEFT OUTER JOIN) Returns all rows from the left along with the matched rows from the right table. If there is no NULL values are returned for columns from the right ensuring unmatched rows from the left table are still included". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1323, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01324', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the following Price) Which SQL command correctly modifies the price of all products in the category by increasing it by 10%?', '["MODIFY Product SET Price = Price * 1.10 WHERE Category =", "UPDATE Product CHANGE Price = Price * 1.10 IF Category =", "UPDATE Product SET Price = Price * 1.10 WHERE Category =", "CHANGE Product SET Price = Price * 1.10 WHERE Category IS", "UPDATE INTO Product SET Price = Price * 1.10 WHERE Category ="]'::jsonb, 3, 'UPDATE This clause specifies the table you want to which is the table. SET Price = Price * 1.10: This clause indicates the column you want to update (''Price'') and the new value it should hold. Multiplying the current Price by 1.10 effectively increases it by 10%', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1324, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01325', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL statement is used to update the City of the customer with = 5 to in the Customers table?', '["UPDATE Customers SET City = WHERE =", "MODIFY Customers SET City = WHERE =", "ALTER TABLE Customers CHANGE City = WHERE =", "CHANGE Customers City = WHERE =", "UPDATE Customers WHERE = 5 SET City ="]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct SQL statement for updating data in a table is the UPDATE statement. This statement modifies existing records in a table. The WHERE clause is crucial to specify which rows to j', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1325, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01326', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Given the following two j.= fol ,nd will be the output of the following SQL Name FROM Customers WHERE NOT IN (SELECT FROM : 3) Explanation', '["Alice", "Bob", "Carol", "3, Carol", "1, Alice 6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "Alice". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1326, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01327', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL command is used to remove all data from a table without deleting the table itself?', '["DROP", "CLEAR", "REMOVE", "ERASE", "TRUNCATE 6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "DROP". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1327, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01328', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the following hireDate) Which of the following SQL statements is syntactically correct?', '["select from", "select employee from", "select * where salary from", "select from employee", "select salary where from"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "select from". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1328, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01329', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which attribute sets the initial value of an input element? wdodk w.h ilik : 2) The value attribute in an <input> element sets the initial value of the input field. When the form is loaded in the value attribute defines what will be displayed inside the input field by default. If a user does not modify this it will be the value submitted with the form. HTML <input> w.h w.h w.h o¾Ykh w.h fkdlrkafka th fmdaruh ,o w.h jkq an explanation of the other', '["The name attribute is used to specify the name of the input element. This name is used to reference the input field''s data when the form is submitted. It does not set the initial value of the input field. ku", "type attribute specifies the type of input element checkbox etc.). It determines the kind of data that the input field is expected to but it does not set the initial value. type j\u00be.h text\" password\" radio\" checkbox", "The placeholder attribute provides a hint to the user about what they should enter in the input field. This text appears inside the input field as a greyed-out prompt until the user starts but it does not set the actual value of the input field. placeholder", "This is not a valid attribute for an input element in There is no initial attribute that sets the value of an input element", "name"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "The name attribute is used to specify the name of the input element. This name is used to reference the input field''s data when the form is submitted. It does not set the initial value of the input field. ku". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1329, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01330', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following SQL statements correctly renames an existing table named old_table to new_table?', '["RENAME TABLE old_table TO", "ALTER TABLE old_table RENAME TO", "CHANGE TABLE old_table TO", "UPDATE TABLE old_table SET NAME =", "MODIFY TABLE old_table TO"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "RENAME TABLE old_table TO". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1330, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01331', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the Location) Which normal form does the relation violate?', '["First Normal Form", "Second Normal Form", "Third Normal Form", "Fourth Normal Form", "None"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The relation has a transitive dependency Emp_ID → Dept_ID (employee belongs to a department) Dept_ID → Location So Dept_Name and Location depend on Dept_ID (a non -key attribute), not directly on the primary key Emp_ID', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1331, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01332', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'You want to change the column name "Age" in table Student to "Stu_Age". Which is the correct SQL command?', '["ALTER TABLE Student RENAME COLUMN Age TO", "UPDATE Student SET Age =", "MODIFY TABLE Student CHANGE Age", "ALTER TABLE Student CHANGE COLUMN Age", "RENAME Age TO Stu_Age IN"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: This is correct in standard SQL and supported by (8.0+). It directly renames the column. Option 2: Wrong. UPDATE is used to change row not column names. UPDATE Option 3: Wrong. MODIFY TABLE is not valid SQL syntax', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1332, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01333', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the purpose of the INNER JOIN clause in SQL?', '["To join all rows from both tables", "To join only unique rows from both tables", "To join only matching rows from both tables", "To join all rows from one table and only matching rows from the other table", "To join rows and remove duplicates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: This would describe a FULL OUTER which includes all rows from both even if there is no match. Option 2: This would be closer to the behavior of DISTINCT (removing duplicates), but INNER JOIN does not remove duplicates inherently', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1333, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01334', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the below table', '["A only. A", "B only. B", "C only. C", "A and C only. A C", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "A only. A". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1334, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01335', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Why are context diagrams important in system analysis?', '["They describe internal algorithms of the system", "They show relationships between tables in a database", "They provide a high-level overview of system scope and boundaries", "They are used to design the user interface layout", "They replace detailed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The key purpose of a context diagram is to show the system as a single process and its interactions with external entities other organizations). It''s all about defining scope and boundaries at a high level', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1335, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01336', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is represented by data flows in a data flow diagram?', '["Just a stream of data", "Only the data going to the database", "Data flows and their directions", "Only data coming from an external entity", "Only the direction of data flow"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The given PHP code initializes a variable $i to 0 and then enters a while loop that continues as long as $i is less than 3. Inside the it prints the current value of $i and then increments $i by 1. The loop executes three first with $i as 0, then 1, and finally 2', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1336, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01337', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following is not a SQL constraint?', '["UNIQUE The UNIQUE constraint ensures that all values in a column are different. It prevents duplicate values in the maintaining data integrity. UNIQUE", "FOREIGN KEY The FOREIGN KEY constraint is used to link two tables together. It ensures that the value in a column (or group of columns) matches the value in another table''s primary key maintaining referential integrity between the tables. FOREIGN KEY j.= folla tlg", "DELETE is not a", "UNIQUE", "FOREIGN KEY"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "DELETE is not a". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1337, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01338', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the appropriate SQL statement for selecting all the data in the record with data starting with the letter "X" in the field in the student table?', '["SELECT * FROM Student WHERE LIKE", "SELECT * FROM Student WHERE =", "SELECT * FROM Student WHERE = ''%X%''", "SELECT * FROM Student WHERE LIKE ''%X%''", "SELECT * FROM Student WHERE LIKE ''X%''"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "SELECT * FROM Student WHERE LIKE ''X%''". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1338, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01339', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which SQL keyword is used to filter records?', '["FILTER BY", "CHECK", "WHERE", "SELECT", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "FILTER BY". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1339, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01340', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following SQL command is not available in the Data Definition Language (DDL)?', '["DROP", "CREATE", "ALTER", "INSERT", "TRUNCATE"]'::jsonb, 4, 'DROP This DDL command used to remove a table or other database object. DDL 2) CREATE This DDL command used to define new database objects such as tables or indexes. DDL 3) ALTER This DDL command used to modify the structure of existing database objects', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1340, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01341', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the following SQL query', '["The query will return the names of students who are enrolled in courses with more than 3 credits", "The query will return all courses that have more than 3 regardless of the student", "The query will return the names of all students", "The query will return only those students whose names start with and are enrolled in courses with more than 3 credits", "The query will return no rows because there are no students enrolled in courses with more than 3 credits. credits 3lg"]'::jsonb, 1, 'SELECT Clause The query selects the Name from the Students table (s) and the Course from the Courses table (c). JOIN Clause It performs an INNER JOIN on the Students and Courses tables based on the ensuring that only matching rows from both tables are returned', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1341, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01342', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the purpose of the method attribute in an form?', '["It specifies the server to send the form data to", "It defines the format of data being submitted", "It sets the method for sending form data", "It sets the database connection method", "It determines the appearance of the form on web page"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The method attribute in an form defines how the form data will be sent to the server. It specifies the method to be used when submitting the form. fmdaruhl th fmdaruh • Appends form data to the URL • Sends form data in the body of the request', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1342, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01343', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following SQL queries will not return the second highest salary from the employees table?', '["SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees WHERE salary", "SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET", "SELECT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT", "SELECT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary LIMIT 1 OFFSET (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT salary) FROM employees) -", "SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "SELECT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary LIMIT 1 OFFSET (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT salary) FROM employees) -". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1343, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01344', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Consider the table below about product sales at a computer accessories Name Price Warrenty_period 1001 Asus ROG Strix RTX 4060 TI 8GB 4 1002 AMD Ryzen 7 8700G 3 1003 ASUS Dual Radeon RX 1 1004 ASUS ROG MAXIMUS Z790 DARK 1 1005 ASUS ROG STRIX Z790 -F GAMING WIFI 2 Consider warranty period is given as jir ,nd nj a customer needed to find the products which offers a warranty period of 2 years or more. Which SQL query retrieves the names of products with corresponding warranty period?', '["SELECT Warrenty_period FROM Products WHERE Warrenty_period", "SELECT Name FROM Products WHERE Warrenty_period =", "SELECT Warrenty_period FROM Products WHERE Warrenty_period =", "SELECT Name FROM Products WHERE Warrenty_period", "SELECT Name FROM Products WHERE Price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Option 1: This query correctly retrieves both the Name and Warranty period from the Products where the Warrenty_period is greater than or equal to 2. This matches the requirement of finding products with at least 2 years of warranty', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1344, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01345', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements best describes data integrity in a database?', '["Ensuring that data is always encrypted", "Ensuring that data is accurate and consistent", "Ensuring that data is always backed up", "Ensuring that data is only accessible to administrators", "Ensuring that data is stored in multiple locations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "Ensuring that data is accurate and consistent". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1345, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01346', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following statements is correct about SQL?', '["Subordinates can only be removed from the table one at a time", "The output of a relational database should always be in the same order as the sublists of a table", "The output of SQL is always a table. SQL", "It is mandatory to replace the data for all parameters while entering the data", "Data selection can be done only through subroutines"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "The output of SQL is always a table. SQL". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1346, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01347', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'Which of the following commands can be used to view the structure of the table?', '["SELECT * FROM", "SHOW SCHEMAS FROM", "DESCRIBE", "STRUCTURE", "TABLE"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "DESCRIBE". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1347, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01348', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the main advantage of using a composite primary key?', '["It provides a more meaningful way to identify records. th", "It allows for complex relationships between tables. th j.=", "It reduces data redundancy th", "It improves query performance th", "It enhances data security th"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A composite primary key is primarily advantageous because it enables the representation of complex relationships between tables in a relational database. This type of primary key consists of two or more columns that together uniquely identify a record', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1348, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01349', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the purpose of the SQL statement?', '["To delete the hire_date column from the employees table. fiajl hire_date", "To modify the data type of the hire_date column in the employees table. fiajl hire_date", "To add a new column named hire_date with the data type DATE to the employees table. fiajl DATE", "To create a new table named employees with a column hire_date", "To retrieve records where the hire_date is not null. hire_date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "To add a new column named hire_date with the data type DATE to the employees table. fiajl DATE". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1349, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01350', 7, 'Database Management Systems', 'What is the primary characteristic of a Star Schema in data warehousing?', '["Fully normalized tables for maximum efficiency", "Use of hierarchical relationships for organizing data", "A schema with no redundancy across tables. j.=", "A central fact table connected to multiple dimension tables. nyqudk j.=", "Direct mapping to OLTP systems for data processing"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "A central fact table connected to multiple dimension tables. nyqudk j.=". This follows from standard relational database concepts/SQL rules applied to the scenario described.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Database Management Systems', NULL, 1350, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
